            <ArticleTitle>[Renal colic: new care in emergency centers].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>256, 258-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The prevalence of renal colic is increasing in industrialized countries, representing a frequent reason for consultation in emergencies. Most patients have simple renal colic that will require analgesia and ambulatory monitoring. Doctors working in emergency centers play a key role in the diagnosis, care and guidance of these patients. They must identify factors of gravity and request urological advice if necessary. This article summarizes the recent diagnostic and therapeutic innovations in the management and guidance of renal colic in emergency centers.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
